{"guide":{"questionsToConsider":["Which request types does your team actually take on, and which should go somewhere else?","How much notice do you need for a campaign compared with a single social post?","Do you want a budget question, or is budget decided elsewhere?","Who on your team triages new requests, and how often?"],"howToUseResponses":"Review new requests on a fixed day each week rather than as they arrive, so priorities are set together instead of by whoever asked loudest. Sort by due date and by whether the date is fixed: a launch-tied deadline outranks a preferred one. Reply to every requester with a decision, and export the responses to CSV each quarter to see which teams ask most and which request types take longest.","customizeSteps":["Edit the request types and channels to match what your team actually produces.","Add a line to the greeting saying how much notice you need, so people know before they start.","Share the link in your team's wiki or intranet, and point every ad hoc request in chat back to it."],"faqs":[{"q":"What should a marketing request form include?","a":"Who is asking, what they need, the audience, the goal and the deadline. Asking whether the deadline is fixed saves a lot of back and forth when two requests clash."},{"q":"How far in advance should people submit marketing requests?","a":"It depends on the work. Many teams ask for a few days for a social post and several weeks for a full campaign; say your own lead times in the greeting."},{"q":"Can different request types ask different questions?","a":"Yes. This form already branches: a campaign request is asked about goals and channels, while a content or design request is asked where the work will be used."},{"q":"Can I add a budget question?","a":"Yes. Use this template copies it into your account, where you can add a number question for budget and edit or remove any other question."}]},"doc":{"schemaVersion":9,"title":"Marketing request","description":"One intake for campaigns,